<RunHelp />

`gimble run WORKFLOW --help` is the full invocation reference. The diagrams below are generated from the same `workflow.Graph` the live console uses. They show the workflow's calls, loops, scopes, and fan-out—not every value assignment, session declaration, or guard.

<h2 id="review">review</h2>

One reviewer inspects a repository read-only and returns a list of concrete correctness findings. The whole workflow is about ten lines, which makes it the one to read first.

```sh
gimble run review --goal "Find correctness bugs in the retry logic"
```

[Read the workflow source](https://github.com/tylergannon/gimble/blob/main/internal/workflows/review/review.go).

<ReviewDiagram />

<h2 id="validate-product">validate-product</h2>

Runs up to three prepared user workloads concurrently, records their browser sessions, asks each tester for a structured UX debrief, checks the screenshots, and synthesizes actionable findings. It is practical user testing—not source review or an exhaustive feature checklist.

```sh
gimble run validate-product \
  --suite-file /abs/user-testing.yaml
```

[Read the workflow source](https://github.com/tylergannon/gimble/blob/main/internal/workflows/validateproduct/validateproduct.go).

<ValidateProductDiagram />

<h2 id="implement">implement</h2>

Works through an ordered list of outcomes. For each outcome, a planner chooses a bounded assignment, a coding agent implements it under a scope coach, and an independent validator judges the result. It advances at 90–95% completion when only small gaps remain. It never commits, pushes, or merges.

```sh
gimble run implement \
  --outcomes-file ./outcomes.json \
  --max-tasks-per-outcome 3
```

Primitives used: [promise loop](../primitives/#promise-loop), [supervision](../primitives/#supervision), [scoped context](../primitives/#context), [`Check`](../primitives/#evidence).

[Read the workflow source](https://github.com/tylergannon/gimble/blob/main/internal/workflows/implementation/implementation.go).

<ImplementDiagram />

<h2 id="research-document">research-document</h2>

Researches a subject into a local semantic index, writes a document within a token budget, and revises it until an independent editor finds only nitpicks. A planner returns five groups of topics, one per parallel researcher; each leaves local source files and an index that cites them.

```sh
gimble run research-document \
  --goal "Explain passkeys to security-conscious product managers" \
  --research-dir ./passkeys-research \
  --output ./passkeys.md \
  --token-budget 4000
```

[Read the workflow source](https://github.com/tylergannon/gimble/blob/main/internal/workflows/researchdocument/researchdocument.go).

<ResearchDocumentDiagram />

<h2 id="pyramid-summary">pyramid-summary</h2>

Compresses one validated document into a pyramid of summaries, halving the token budget at each level until the next would be under 100 tokens. Six authors write levels in parallel; one editor then reads every level together for fidelity and useful compression. Roles can sit on different harnesses in the same run:

```sh
gimble run pyramid-summary \
  --goal "Explain passkeys to security-conscious product managers" \
  --semantic-index ./passkeys-research/INDEX.md \
  --largest-document ./passkeys.md \
  --output-dir ./passkeys-pyramid \
  --document-authoring gpt-5.6-sol:high \
  --editorial-review claude-opus-5:high
```

[Read the workflow source](https://github.com/tylergannon/gimble/blob/main/internal/workflows/pyramidsummary/pyramidsummary.go).

<PyramidSummaryDiagram />

<h2 id="flags">Flags every workflow has</h2>

| Flag | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `--work-dir` | The project directory. Its `.gimble` holds the run. |
| `--ROLE` | One per role the workflow names, as `model` or `model:effort`. Defaults come from one shared file. |
| `--port`, `--uds`, `--no-web` | Where the console listens, or that it should not. |

</DocsPage>
